          "text": "{\"regions\":{\"r2\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"this:\u2013Once upon a time, a Br\u00e1hma\u1e47a[*], who had come by a fruit,\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"which conferred immortality on any one who ate it, gave it to\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"Bhart\u1e5bhari, as the worthiest person to receive it, being the pro-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"tector of many people. Bhart\u1e5bhari gave it to his wife who gave\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"it to her paramour, who in his turn gave it to a sweet-heart of\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"his, who again presented it to Bhart\u1e5bhari. The sloka, however,\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"is quite out of place here as it has no connection with any\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"thing that follows. If it be supposed that it is the expression\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":9,\"text\":\"of the abhorrence of the intrigues and sins of the world felt by\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":10,\"text\":\"some one disgusted with it, then the most natural place for it\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":11,\"text\":\"would be the Vaira'gyas'ataka. Many manuscripts, however,\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":12,\"text\":\"agree in giving it here.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null},\"r3\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"\u091a\u093f\u0928\u094d\u0924\u092f\u093e\u092e\u093f\u2014think with affectionate regard or love. \u092e\u092f\u093f \u0935\u093f\u0930\u0915\u094d\u0924\u093e-\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"Words implying love, attachment ( or disattachment ) &c.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"govern the loc. of the person or thing for whom or which the\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"feeling is shown. Mr. Telang considers the loc. to be \u0935\u0948\u0937\u092f\u093f\u0915\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"being equivalent to \u092e\u0926\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0937\u092f\u0915\u093e\u0928\u0941\u0930\u093e\u0917\u0935\u0924\u0940. \u0905\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0938\u0915\u094d\u0924\u0903\u2014\u0905\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0938\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0902 \u0938\u0915\u094d\u0924\u0903\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"attached or devoted to. \u0905\u0928\u094d\u092f\u093e becomes \u0905\u0928\u094d\u092f i. e. assumes \u092a\u0941\u0902\u0935\u0926\u094d\u092d\u093e\u0935\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"by the V\u00e1rt \u0938\u0930\u094d\u0935\u0928\u093e\u092e\u094d\u0928\u0903 \u0938\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0930\u0940\u092e\u093e\u0924\u094d\u0930\u0947 ( in Sam\u00e2sa and the other V\u1e5bttis )\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"\u092a\u0941\u0902\u0935\u0926\u094d\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0903 See Malli. on \u0905\u0928\u0928\u094d\u092f\u092d\u093e\u091c\u0902 \u092a\u0924\u093f\u092e\u093e\u092a\u094d\u0928\u0941\u0939\u0940\u0924\u093f &c. Kum. III. 63.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":9,\"text\":\"\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"Some Sanskrit forms and the punctuation after \u0935\u093f\u0930\u0915\u094d\u0924\u093e are difficult to read.\"},\"r4\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"\u0915\u0943\u0924\u0947\u2014For, for the sake of ( Indec. ) is often compounded as\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"here. Cf. \u0915\u093e\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u092f\u0936\u0938\u0947\u093d\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0915\u0943\u0924\u0947 &c. Kav. P. I. When not compounded\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"it governs the gen; \u0905\u092e\u0940\u0937\u093e\u0902 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u093e\u0923\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0902 \u0915\u0943\u0924\u0947 Vair. S'a. 36. \u092a\u0930\u093f\u0936\u0941\u0937\u094d\u092f\u0924\u093f\u2014\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"pines for, feels ardent love for. This reading is evidently prefer-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"able ( as Mr. Telang also thinks ) to \u092a\u0930\u093f\u0924\u0941\u0937\u094d\u092f\u0924\u093f which may be\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"best rendered as \u201c delighted with all I do. \u201d\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null},\"r5\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"\u0927\u093f\u0915\u094d\u2014is used with the acc. \u092e\u0926\u0928\u2014from \u092e\u0926\u094d ( \u092e\u093e\u0926\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0928\u0947\u0928 ) + \u0905\u0928\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"Lit. the intoxicating god. \u091a \u0907\u092e\u093e\u092e\u094d\u2014The hiatus here is allowed\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"by the rule \u0938\u0902\u0939\u093f\u0924\u0948\u0915\u092a\u0926\u0947 &c. Sid. Kau.; see com.\u2014Sandhi is\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"absolutely necessary in a grammatical form, in the case of a\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"preposition and a root, as also in compounds; but in composi-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Sanskrit derivation at the end of the first line is blurred.\"},\"r6\":{\"type\":\"footnote\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"* According to another version king Vicrama obtained the fruit\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"from a Yogin, he gave it to a Br\u00e0hmana, he to Bhart\u1e5bhari and so on.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null},\"r1\":{\"type\":\"page_number\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"( 3 )\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null}},\"units\":[{\"id\":\"u1\",\"type\":\"notes\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"u2\"]},{\"id\":\"u2\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"r2\",\"r3\",\"r4\",\"r5\"]},{\"id\":\"u3\",\"type\":\"footnote\",\"number\":null,\"label\":\"*\",\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r6\"]}],\"flows\":[{\"flow\":\"main\",\"units\":[\"u1\"]},{\"flow\":\"notes\",\"units\":[\"u3\"]}],\"relations\":[{\"type\":\"note\",\"region\":\"r2\",\"line\":1,\"marker\":\"*\",\"to\":\"u3\"}]}"
